<artifact id="review">

<task>
Perform a comprehensive code review of the current change and write `review.md`.
Review the post-build candidate snapshot, not the intended plan.
</task>

<dependencies>
Read the issue, proposal, specs, design, tasks, and all changed files before producing the final review.
</dependencies>

<instruction>
Perform a full review pass. Do not stop after the first blocker.

Inspect:
1. Current issue acceptance criteria with concrete evidence.
2. All changed files.
3. Adjacent retry, recovery, and artifact paths.
4. Regression coverage gaps.
5. Cross-cutting concerns: security, data safety, public contracts, migration impact.
</instruction>

<review_dimensions>
- Correctness: logic errors, bugs, edge cases.
- Complexity: unnecessary coupling, overly large functions, confusing flow.
- Test Coverage: new code has meaningful tests and all relevant tests pass.
- Security: input validation, no injection risks, no exposed secrets.
- Spec Compliance: acceptance criteria are verified with file paths, lines, or actual values.
</review_dimensions>

<repair_policy>
You MAY repair small, local, low-risk issues directly during review:
- formatting
- typos
- obvious missing guards
- small test expectation updates
- import cleanup
- dead code removal

You MUST NOT repair issues involving:
- product behavior changes
- public contract changes
- data safety or security posture changes
- architectural judgment
- broad refactoring
- ambiguous trade-offs

If unsure, report the issue as unresolved instead of fixing it.
</repair_policy>

<output_format>
# Review Report

## Result: PASS / FAIL

## Repaired Items

- [ID: item-N]
  Severity: info
  Scope: formatting | typos | missing-obvious-guards | ...
  Evidence: What was wrong and what was changed
  Verification: Command run to verify the fix
  Status: resolved

## Blocking Items

- [ID: item-N]
  Severity: blocking
  Scope: file path or area
  Evidence: What is wrong [disallowed:reason] if repair was considered but disallowed
  SuggestedAction: What should be done
  Verification: How to verify the fix
  Status: open | unresolved

## Follow-up Items

- [ID: item-N]
  Severity: follow-up
  Scope: file path or area
  Evidence: Description
  SuggestedAction: Suggested improvement
  Status: follow-up

## Pre-existing or Out-of-scope Items

- [ID: item-N]
  Severity: info | warning
  Scope: file path or area
  Evidence: Description
  SuggestedAction: Optional future action
  Status: pre-existing | out-of-scope
</output_format>

<verdict_rules>
- The verdict MUST reflect the post-repair candidate snapshot.
- Do not PASS if unresolved blocking items remain.
- Do not PASS if repaired items lack verification evidence.
- Pre-existing failures do not block the current change but must be reported.
</verdict_rules>

<verdict_marker>
The final line of `review.md` MUST be exactly one of:
- `<promise>PASS</promise>` if review passes.
- `<promise>FAIL</promise>` if review fails.

Do NOT include more than one marker. Do NOT include markers in examples or quoted text.
</verdict_marker>

<output>
Write to: `${{ openspecChangeDir }}/review.md`
</output>

<path_contract>
Use `${{ openspecChangeDir }}` exactly as the change directory. Do not create or use a title slug, issue-title directory, numeric-title directory, or any other `openspec/changes/*` path for this artifact.
</path_contract>

</artifact>
